{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-0a97204b-2da2-4022-a8cc-4b6e24687cbe","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-944ba9a14160467a","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8004-kkcvpu4k/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-decomposition.md","content":"# Decomposition Summary: RHAISTRAT-8004 — Default Vector Store for Gen AI Studio\n\nRHAISTRAT-8004 delivers a default, out-of-the-box vector store (PostgreSQL with pgvector) so that\nGen AI Studio playground RAG workflows continue to function when inline providers are removed from\nthe OGX (Llama Stack) distribution per RHAISTRAT-7474. The strategy is Blocker priority and\nstatus Release Pending.\n\n**Input fidelity note:** the full strategy text exceeds the Jira description size limit and is\nstored as an attachment (`RHAISTRAT-8004-strategy.md`) that is not present in the pre-provisioned\nworkspace. This decomposition is derived from the Jira issue body (Summary, Problem Statement,\nAcceptance Criteria, Success Criteria, Open Questions) plus the detailed Staff Engineer Input,\nwhich takes precedence per Step 1.5. The Affected Components table, Impacted Teams table,\nprioritized High Level Requirements, Dependencies table, and Non-Functional Requirements from the\nattachment were unavailable; requirement traceability below maps the strategy's Acceptance\nCriteria (post Staff-Engineer filtering) plus Staff-Engineer-derived requirements instead of HLR\nIDs. See Health Warnings.\n\n## Epic List\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Component | Team | Priority |\n|----|-------|------|-----------|------|----------|\n| RHAISTRAT-8004-E001 | Default vector store provisioning approach go/no-go decision | Investigation | Gen AI Studio | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8004-E002 | Enable and validate the remote pgvector vector_io provider in the OGX distribution | Implementation | OGX Core | OGX Core | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8004-E003 | Configure Gen AI Studio playground OGXServer instances to use a pgvector vector store | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8004-E004 | Benchmark pgvector against the inline Milvus baseline for playground RAG and recommend resource sizing | Investigation | RAG + Vector DB | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8004-E005 | Document the default vector store, bring-your-own configuration, and migration path | Implementation (docs-authoring) | Documentation | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E006 | Operator-based PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning for Gen AI Studio (go outcome) | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E006 | BFF-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning for Gen AI Studio playgrounds (no-go outcome) | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n\nEpic count: 7 total (5 main-plan epics + 2 branch epics). Branch epics are conditional outcomes of\nthe E001 go/no-go decision and are attached to the gating Investigation as a plan document; they\nare not standalone main-plan nodes.\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[\"E001 Provisioning approach go/no-go (Investigation)\"]\n    E002[\"E002 OGX distribution pgvector provider\"]\n    E003[\"E003 Playground pgvector wiring\"]\n    E004[\"E004 pgvector benchmark and sizing\"]\n    E005[\"E005 Documentation\"]\n\n    E001 --> BA[\"BRANCH-A-E006 Operator-based provisioning (branch A)\"]\n    E001 --> BB[\"BRANCH-B-E006 BFF-managed provisioning (branch B)\"]\n    E001 --> E005\n    E002 --> E005\n    E003 --> E005\n\n    E004 -.-> BA\n    E004 -.-> BB\n```\n\nSolid arrows are blocking DAG edges recorded in frontmatter `dependencies`. Dashed arrows are\ninformational flows only (benchmark sizing results feed the provisioning epics as acceptance\ncriteria / tuning input — no blocking edge; sizing starts from the model-registry-operator\nreference values per Staff Engineer Input). E001, E002, E003, and E004 are all roots and may start\nin parallel (Tier 1).\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E001 → BRANCH-A-E006 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The go/no-go decision determines whether operator-based provisioning is built at all; true gate with `remove` semantics for the branch if the outcome is \"no-go\". |\n| E001 → BRANCH-B-E006 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | BFF-managed provisioning only exists if the no-go outcome selects it; true gate with `remove` semantics for the branch if the outcome is \"go\". |\n| E001 → E005 | Rule 3 / Rule 11 | Documentation must describe the provisioning approach that was actually selected (operator-based, BFF-managed, or bring-your-own); the decision rewrites the doc content. |\n| E002 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring blocked by all Implementations) | Docs describe the delivered pgvector provider capability. |\n| E003 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring blocked by all Implementations) | Docs describe the delivered playground wiring and user-facing behavior. |\n| E004 ⇢ (dashed, no edge) provisioning epics | Rule 4 / AC gate | Benchmark results are informational for sizing (start from model-registry-operator reference; adjust per benchmark). Failure means tune-and-retry, not different epics. |\n| No edge E002 → E003 | Rule 13 carve-out | The distribution's pgvector provider is consumed by the playground via configuration (activation env var / CR spec), not a build-against artifact; the contract already exists (`ENABLE_PGVECTOR`, remote::pgvector). Wired as an acceptance criterion on E003 (\"validated against an OGX distribution build that includes the remote pgvector provider\"), not a DAG edge. |\n| No edge E001 → E002/E003/E004 | Step 5.5 Tier 1 | pgvector enablement, playground wiring, and benchmarking deliver independent value with any PostgreSQL endpoint (including bring-your-own); they are not gated by the provisioning decision. |\n| No edge konflux-onboarding / repo-onboarding / license-validation | Rules 5–10 (not applicable) | Staff Engineer Correction 2: the Red Hat `rhel9/postgresql-16` image already includes pgvector and is already referenced by the rhods-operator CSV (`RELATED_IMAGE_POSTGRESQL_16_IMAGE`) for disconnected installs. No new image build, no new repo, no Konflux onboarding chain. |\n| No edge from branch epics to E005 | Conditional-decomposition rule 2 | Main-plan epics must not depend on branch epics; docs cover the selected approach generically and the branch epics carry provisioning-specific documentation as scope/AC. |\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\nThe HLR table from the strategy attachment was unavailable. Requirements are derived from the\nstrategy's Acceptance Criteria (numbered AC-n, after Staff-Engineer filtering) and\nStaff-Engineer-derived requirements (numbered SE-n):\n\n| Requirement | Text (abridged) | Epics |\n|-------------|-----------------|-------|\n| AC-1 | Default vector store available out-of-the-box, no manual provisioning/configuration | BRANCH-A-E006, BRANCH-B-E006 |\n| AC-2 | Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows function with the default vector store, no loss of capability | E002, E003, E004 |\n| AC-3 (removed) | Auto Rag functions with the default vector store | Removed from scope per Staff Engineer Input (Auto Rag compatibility is RHAISTRAT-7824 scope, targeting RHOAI 3.6) — see Overrides |\n| AC-4 | Vector store runs as a separate process, decoupled from the OGX instance (not inline) | E002, E003 |\n| AC-5 | Scoped appropriately for multi-tenant environments (per-namespace isolation) | E001 (decision), BRANCH-A-E006, BRANCH-B-E006 |\n| AC-6 | Lightweight enough for resource-constrained environments | E004 (sizing), BRANCH-A-E006, BRANCH-B-E006 |\n| SE-1 | Self-provisioning pattern follows the model-registry-operator auto-provisioned PostgreSQL pattern; no custom image build | BRANCH-A-E006 |\n| SE-2 | Bring-your-own PostgreSQL supported via user-configured connection secret in the OGXServer CR spec | E003, E005 |\n| SE-3 | Migration path from the default vector store to a production-grade vector database (align with commonly-used providers) | E003 (configurable connection), E005 (documentation) |\n| SE-4 | Portability: consume a platform-provided shared connection string when the DSC-managed shared PostgreSQL arrives (RHOAI 3.6) | E003, BRANCH-B-E006 |\n| SE-5 | Vector storage (vector_io) configuration only; OGX application storage remains SQLite — no storage migration | E003 (constraint) |\n| SE-6 | No additional manual setup steps for RAG workflows vs the current experience | BRANCH-A-E006, BRANCH-B-E006, E003 |\n| SE-7 | Benchmark captures inline Milvus baseline; pgvector within same order of magnitude (dev-experimentation scope, no strict SLAs; flat scan acceptable) | E004 |\n| SE-8 | Resource sizing starts from model-registry-operator PostgreSQL requests/limits, adjusted per benchmark | BRANCH-A-E006, BRANCH-B-E006, E004 |\n\nAll post-filtering requirements are covered; no orphans.\n\n## Conditional Branches\n\nThe go/no-go decision (E001) has bounded outcomes (≤3) that change which provisioning\nImplementation exists:\n\n- **Branch A — \"go\"**: RHAISTRAT-7164 (dashboard operator modularization) delivers the\n  RHOAI-controlled operator layer in time. PostgreSQL/pgvector lifecycle management is implemented\n  in that operator following the model-registry-operator pattern. External precondition: the\n  RHAISTRAT-7164 operator layer must actually land; it is tracked by E001 and is not an epic in\n  this strategy. → `RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E006.md`\n- **Branch B — \"no-go, BFF-managed\"**: RHAISTRAT-7164 does not land in time, and the gen-ai BFF\n  provisions the PostgreSQL/pgvector instance directly (Deployment, Service, PVC, Secret) when a\n  playground with RAG is configured — the same pattern the BFF already uses for guardrails\n  infrastructure. → `RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E006.md`\n- **Branch C — \"no-go, bri