{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-06c7e85b-ee68-43a0-b1d3-0c6b48fde9a3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-901254392eb553c7","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8004-kkcvpu4k/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E006.md","content":"# BFF-Managed PostgreSQL/pgvector Provisioning for Gen AI Studio Playgrounds (No-Go Outcome)\n\n## Description\n\nBranch outcome \"no-go\": the dashboard operator modularization (RHAISTRAT-7164) does not deliver\nthe RHOAI-controlled operator layer in time for the target release, and the go/no-go decision\nselects BFF-managed provisioning as the fallback. The gen-ai BFF provisions the\nPostgreSQL/pgvector instance directly when a playground with RAG is configured and no suitable\nPostgreSQL connection already exists in the namespace — the same pattern the BFF already uses to\nprovision guardrails infrastructure. The BFF creates the PostgreSQL Deployment, Service, PVC, and\nSecret using the existing Red Hat `rhel9/postgresql-16` image (which includes pgvector out of the\nbox — no custom image build). Because the BFF is not an operator with a reconciliation loop, the\nlifecycle caveats flagged by the Staff Engineer Input (cleanup on playground deletion, pod\nrestart handling, credential rotation) require explicit design attention in this epic.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Implement BFF-managed provisioning of a PostgreSQL/pgvector instance (Deployment, Service,\n  PVC, credentials Secret) when a playground with RAG is configured and no PostgreSQL connection\n  secret satisfying the wiring contract already exists in the namespace.\n- Use the existing Red Hat PostgreSQL image with pgvector; no custom image build.\n- Design for portability: when the platform-provided shared PostgreSQL becomes available (planned\n  RHOAI 3.6), the BFF consumes the shared connection string instead of self-provisioning — the\n  provisioning path must be cleanly replaceable by configuration.\n- Explicitly design and handle lifecycle management without a reconciliation loop:\n  - cleanup of provisioned resources when the playground is deleted;\n  - behavior on BFF pod restarts (idempotent reconciliation of expected resources);\n  - credential handling and any rotation strategy.\n- Scope provisioned instances per-namespace (the playground's namespace), matching the confirmed\n  scoping model.\n- Set resource requests/limits starting from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL reference\n  values, adjusted per benchmark findings.\n- Document the provisioning behavior and its limits for inclusion in the user-facing\n  documentation.\n- If, during design, the lifecycle caveats prove unviable, escalate back through the go/no-go\n  decision to the bring-your-own posture (user-configured PostgreSQL via the OGXServer CR spec,\n  covered by the wiring work and documentation rather than auto-provisioning).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A Gen AI Studio playground with RAG enabled gets a working PostgreSQL/pgvector instance\n  provisioned automatically by the BFF, with no manual user setup, when no user-supplied\n  PostgreSQL configuration exists.\n- The provisioned instance uses the existing Red Hat PostgreSQL image with pgvector (no custom\n  image build).\n- The connection secret satisfies the contract consumed by the playground OGXServer vector_io\n  wiring, and playground RAG works end-to-end against the provisioned instance.\n- Provisioned resources are cleaned up when the playground is deleted; BFF pod restarts do not\n  cause duplicate or broken PostgreSQL resources (idempotent handling is demonstrated).\n- Credential handling (creation and any rotation approach) is designed and documented.\n- The provisioning path is replaceable by a shared connection string with configuration changes\n  only (portability demonstrated or validated by design review).\n- Instances are scoped per-namespace — tenants in different namespaces do not share vector data.\n- Resource requests/limits start from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL reference values\n  and are recorded with rationale (benchmark-adjusted where available).\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- AC-1 (default vector store available out-of-the-box, no manual provisioning).\n- AC-2 (playground RAG workflows function with the default vector store) — provisioning completes\n  the end-to-end path.\n- AC-5 (multi-tenant scoping, per-namespace isolation).\n- AC-6 (lightweight for resource-constrained environments) — resource sizing.\n- SE-4 (portability to the platform-provided shared connection string in RHOAI 3.6).\n- SE-6 (no additional manual setup steps).\n- SE-8 (resource sizing from model-registry-operator reference).\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":79566,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b30c0eb90956327c","agent_id":"abdf0a0fcd4c2ca8d","session_id":"546378e3-e46e-4fda-8937-a306cf6cc87c","uuid":"445f4366-eb32-42ad-a4fc-3c34b89b99bd","timestamp":"2026-10-21T19:06:14.340Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8004"}
