{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b32c2e295588d0a6","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E006\n3\ttitle: BFF-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning for Gen AI Studio playgrounds\n4\t  (no-go outcome)\n5\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n6\tcomponent: Gen AI Studio\n7\tteam: Gen AI Studio\n8\ttype: Implementation\n9\tpriority: P0\n10\tbranch: B\n11\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n12\tdependencies:\n13\t- RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n14\tgate_failure_impact:\n15\t  action: remove\n16\t  fallback_approach: If the go/no-go decision selects operator-based provisioning\n17\t    (RHAISTRAT-7164 lands), this epic is removed; if BFF-managed provisioning proves\n18\t    unviable during lifecycle design, fall back to the bring-your-own PostgreSQL posture\n19\t    (user-configured OGXServer CR spec connection) with no auto-provisioning epic.\n20\tai_signals:\n21\t  change_specificity: 1\n22\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n23\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n24\t  existing_foundation: 1\n25\t  open_questions: -1\n26\t  external_dependency: 0\n27\t  human_process_gates: 0\n28\t  repo_access: 1\n29\t  architecture_claims: 1\n30\timplementation_type: null\n31\tinvestigation_signals: null\n32\tai_implementability: High\n33\tai_implementability_score: 5\n34\tjira_key: null\n35\t---\n36\t# BFF-Managed PostgreSQL/pgvector Provisioning for Gen AI Studio Playgrounds (No-Go Outcome)\n37\t\n38\t## Description\n39\t\n40\tBranch outcome \"no-go\": the dashboard operator modularization (RHAISTRAT-7164) does not deliver\n41\tthe RHOAI-controlled operator layer in time for the target release, and the go/no-go decision\n42\tselects BFF-managed provisioning as the fallback. The gen-ai BFF provisions the\n43\tPostgreSQL/pgvector instance directly when a playground with RAG is configured and no suitable\n44\tPostgreSQL connection already exists in the namespace — the same pattern the BFF already uses to\n45\tprovision guardrails infrastructure. The BFF creates the PostgreSQL Deployment, Service, PVC, and\n46\tSecret using the existing Red Hat `rhel9/postgresql-16` image (which includes pgvector out of the\n47\tbox — no custom image build). Because the BFF is not an operator with a reconciliation loop, the\n48\tlifecycle caveats flagged by the Staff Engineer Input (cleanup on playground deletion, pod\n49\trestart handling, credential rotation) require explicit design attention in this epic.\n50\t\n51\t## Scope\n52\t\n53\t- Implement BFF-managed provisioning of a PostgreSQL/pgvector instance (Deployment, Service,\n54\t  PVC, credentials Secret) when a playground with RAG is configured and no PostgreSQL connection\n55\t  secret satisfying the wiring contract already exists in the namespace.\n56\t- Use the existing Red Hat PostgreSQL image with pgvector; no custom image build.\n57\t- Design for portability: when the platform-provided shared PostgreSQL becomes available (planned\n58\t  RHOAI 3.6), the BFF consumes the shared connection string instead of self-provisioning — the\n59\t  provisioning path must be cleanly replaceable by configuration.\n60\t- Explicitly design and handle lifecycle management without a reconciliation loop:\n61\t  - cleanup of provisioned resources when the playground is deleted;\n62\t  - behavior on BFF pod restarts (idempotent reconciliation of expected resources);\n63\t  - credential handling and any rotation strategy.\n64\t- Scope provisioned instances per-namespace (the playground's namespace), matching the confirmed\n65\t  scoping model.\n66\t- Set resource requests/limits starting from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL reference\n67\t  values, adjusted per benchmark findings.\n68\t- Document the provisioning behavior and its limits for inclusion in the user-facing\n69\t  documentation.\n70\t- If, during design, the lifecycle caveats prove unviable, escalate back through the go/no-go\n71\t  decision to the bring-your-own posture (user-configured PostgreSQL via the OGXServer CR spec,\n72\t  covered by the wiring work and documentation rather than auto-provisioning).\n73\t\n74\t## Acceptance Criteria\n75\t\n76\t- A Gen AI Studio playground with RAG enabled gets a working PostgreSQL/pgvector instance\n77\t  provisioned automatically by the BFF, with no manual user setup, when no user-supplied\n78\t  PostgreSQL configuration exists.\n79\t- The provisioned instance uses the existing Red Hat PostgreSQL image with pgvector (no custom\n80\t  image build).\n81\t- The connection secret satisfies the contract consumed by the playground OGXServer vector_io\n82\t  wiring, and playground RAG works end-to-end against the provisioned instance.\n83\t- Provisioned resources are cleaned up when the playground is deleted; BFF pod restarts do not\n84\t  cause duplicate or broken PostgreSQL resources (idempotent handling is demonstrated).\n85\t- Credential handling (creation and any rotation approach) is designed and documented.\n86\t- The provisioning path is replaceable by a shared connection string with configuration changes\n87\t  only (portability demonstrated or validated by design review).\n88\t- Instances are scoped per-namespace — tenants in different namespaces do not share vector data.\n89\t- Resource requests/limits start from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL reference values\n90\t  and are recorded with rationale (benchmark-adjusted where available).\n91\t\n92\t## HLR Traceability\n93\t\n94\t- AC-1 (default vector store available out-of-the-box, no manual provisioning).\n95\t- AC-2 (playground RAG workflows function with the default vector store) — provisioning completes\n96\t  the end-to-end path.\n97\t- AC-5 (multi-tenant scoping, per-namespace isolation).\n98\t- AC-6 (lightweight for resource-constrained environments) — resource sizing.\n99\t- SE-4 (portability to the platform-provided shared connection string in RHOAI 3.6).\n100\t- SE-6 (no additional manual setup steps).\n101\t- SE-8 (resource sizing from model-registry-operator reference).\n102\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a5d9e3a32beb111a","agent_id":"a9d1510e518acea17","session_id":"546378e3-e46e-4fda-8937-a306cf6cc87c","uuid":"bc0d12ba-31e1-402f-8f45-170de0a9b043","timestamp":"2026-10-21T19:09:51.964Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b32c2e295588d0a6","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
