validation epics exist, so the rule 7/8/10\n  parallelism constraints do not apply (and the Staff Engineer's Correction 2 — the\n  pgvector-capable image already exists — justifies their absence).\n\n### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n\nEach epic has a single component/team tuple, and distinct tuples produce distinct epics: E002 is\nOGX Core/OGX Core (distribution work), E001/E003/branches are Gen AI Studio, E004 is\nRAG + Vector DB executed by the Gen AI Studio team, E005 is Documentation. All component names\nexist in `.context/rhai-components.txt`, and all referenced repos (odh-dashboard,\nogx-distribution, ogx-k8s-operator, model-registry-operator, rhods-operator) have architecture\ncontext files.\n\nNo scope duplication: E002 (provider enablement in the distribution), E003 (BFF/OGXServer\nwiring), and the branch epics (PostgreSQL provisioning) are disjoint surfaces with a clean\ninterface between them (the connection-secret contract defined in E003 and consumed by the\nbranches). The two branch epics overlap by design — they are mutually exclusive conditional\noutcomes, not sibling duplication. Epic sizes are focused; nothing approaches multi-sprint scope.\nE005 correctly separates content authoring from code implementation.\n\nMinor: the branch epics each carry a \"document the provisioning behavior\" scope bullet that\noverlaps E005's user-facing documentation scope. This is structurally forced (main-plan docs\ncannot depend on branch epics) and the split is deliberate — the branch contributes technical\ninput while E005 authors the user-facing topics — but E005's publication step should explicitly\nincorporate the selected branch's provisioning documentation.\n\n### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n\nE001 is a genuine Investigation: its go/no-go outcome determines which provisioning\nImplementation exists (branch A, branch B, or the no-epic bring-your-own fallback), and it\nresolves the scoping-model open question. It has downstream dependents and both branch epics are