{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9f9a57e06586b6b0","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8004\n3\tscore: 14\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: minor\n8\t  criterion: HLR Coverage\n9\t  description: Strategy attachment (RHAISTRAT-8004-strategy.md) with the HLR/NFR/component\n10\t    tables is unavailable; traceability uses derived AC-n/SE-n requirement IDs instead\n11\t    of HLR IDs. Documented in Health Warnings, but a human should reconcile the decomposition\n12\t    against the attachment before execution.\n13\t- severity: minor\n14\t  criterion: Epic Boundaries\n15\t  description: Branch epics each carry a provisioning-documentation scope bullet overlapping\n16\t    E005 docs scope (structurally forced because main-plan docs cannot depend on branch\n17\t    epics); E005 publication should explicitly incorporate the selected branch provisioning\n18\t    documentation.\n19\t- severity: minor\n20\t  criterion: Type Correctness\n21\t  description: E004 is typed Investigation but no epic depends on or is gated by it\n22\t    - its sizing output flows only via informational links and AC wording. Typing\n23\t    is defensible (resolves pgvector-acceptability uncertainty and sizing), but the\n24\t    benchmark harness portion could arguably be an Implementation with analysis as\n25\t    an AC.\n26\terror: null\n27\t---\n28\t# Decomposition Review: RHAISTRAT-8004\n29\t\n30\t## Review Summary\n31\t\n32\tScore: 14/14 — pass\n33\tRecommendation: accept\n34\t\n35\t## Criterion Details\n36\t\n37\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n38\t\n39\tThe strategy file contains no HLR table — the full AI-generated strategy exceeds the Jira\n40\tdescription limit and lives in an attachment (`RHAISTRAT-8004-strategy.md`) that is not present\n41\tin the workspace. The decomposition handles this transparently: it derives requirements from the\n42\tstrategy's Acceptance Criteria (AC-1..AC-6, after Staff-Engineer filtering) and Staff-Engineer\n43\tdirectives (SE-1..SE-8), and builds a complete traceability matrix mapping every requirement to\n44\tat least one epic. AC-3 (Auto Rag) is removed with explicit justification per the Staff Engineer\n45\tInput (Auto Rag compatibility is RHAISTRAT-7824 scope, RHOAI 3.6) — a documented override, not a\n46\tsilent drop. All remaining ACs and SE requirements map to epics; no orphans.\n47\t\n48\tPriority handling is correct: the strategy is Blocker priority and all epics are P0, so there is\n49\tno priority inheritance violation and no priority collapse (no deferrable lower-priority features\n50\twere bundled — everything is P0 by strategy priority). E005 is `docs-authoring`; its P0 priority\n51\tis consistent with the Blocker→P0 mapping and it is exempt from inheritance checks anyway.\n52\tStrategy priority is Release Pending/Blocker and the release-commitment risk around the\n53\tRHAISTRAT-7164 dependency is surfaced as a health warning rather than buried.\n54\t\n55\tMinor (input fidelity): because the HLR/NFR/component tables were unavailable, traceability uses\n56\tderived requirement IDs instead of HLR IDs. This is documented in Health Warnings with a\n57\trecommendation for human reconciliation — acceptable, but a reconciler should confirm no\n58\tattachment HLR is unaccounted for.\n59\t\n60\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n61\t\n62\tCross-checked every edge in both directions:\n63\t\n64\t- Frontmatter `dependencies` match the summary DAG diagram exactly: E005 depends on\n65\t  [E001, E002, E003]; both branch epics depend on [E001]; E001–E004 have no dependencies.\n66\t  Arrows run dependency → dependent, consistent with the stated convention.\n67\t- No circular dependencies; no edge references a nonexistent epic.\n68\t- Branch files: both `RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E006` and `-BRANCH-B-E006` set `branch` (A/B,\n69\t  matching filename) and `gated_by: RHAISTRAT-8004-E001`, and the `gated_by` target appears in\n70\t  each branch's direct `dependencies`. No main-plan epic lists a branch epic in its\n71\t  `dependencies` — E005 correctly does not depend on the branches, and the branch-specific\n72\t  provisioning documentation lives inside the branches to compensate.\n73\t- The E002 → E003 non-edge is correctly handled: the playground consumes the distribution's\n74\t  pgvector provider via configuration (`ENABLE_PGVECTOR`, `remote::pgvector` — a contract that\n75\t  already exists), so it is wired as an acceptance criterion on E003 (\"validated against an OGX\n76\t  distribution build that includes the remote pgvector provider\") rather than a `dependencies`\n77\t  edge. This is exactly the Rule 13 configuration-reference treatment.\n78\t- The E004 dashed (informational) edges to the provisioning epics are justified: sizing starts\n79\t  from the model-registry-operator reference values per Staff input, with benchmark results as\n80\t  tuning input; failure means tune-and-retry, not different epics. No data dependency exists that\n81\t  requires a blocking edge.\n82\t- Parallelism: E001, E002, E003, E004 are all roots (Tier 1) — naturally parallel work is not\n83\t  serialized. Critical path length 2 is reasonable for this strategy size. No\n84\t  konflux-onboarding/repo-onboarding/license-validation epics exist, so the rule 7/8/10\n85\t  parallelism constraints do not apply (and the Staff Engineer's Correction 2 — the\n86\t  pgvector-capable image already exists — justifies their absence).\n87\t\n88\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n89\t\n90\tEach epic has a single component/team tuple, and distinct tuples produce distinct epics: E002 is\n91\tOGX Core/OGX Core (distribution work), E001/E003/branches are Gen AI Studio, E004 is\n92\tRAG + Vector DB executed by the Gen AI Studio team, E005 is Documentation. All component names\n93\texist in `.context/rhai-components.txt`, and all referenced repos (odh-dashboard,\n94\togx-distribution, ogx-k8s-operator, model-registry-operator, rhods-operator) have architecture\n95\tcontext files.\n96\t\n97\tNo scope duplication: E002 (provider enablement in the distribution), E003 (BFF/OGXServer\n98\twiring), and the branch epics (PostgreSQL provisioning) are disjoint surfaces with a clean\n99\tinterface between them (the connection-secret contract defined in E003 and consumed by the\n100\tbranches). The two branch epics overlap by design — they are mutually exclusive conditional\n101\toutcomes, not sibling duplication. Epic sizes are focused; nothing approaches multi-sprint scope.\n102\tE005 correctly separates content authoring from code implementation.\n103\t\n104\tMinor: the branch epics each carry a \"document the provisioning behavior\" scope bullet that\n105\toverlaps E005's user-facing documentation scope. This is structurally forced (main-plan docs\n106\tcannot depend on branch epics) and the split is deliberate — the branch contributes technical\n107\tinput while E005 authors the user-facing topics — but E005's publication step should explicitly\n108\tincorporate the selected branch's provisioning documentation.\n109\t\n110\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n111\t\n112\tE001 is a genuine Investigation: its go/no-go outcome determines which provisioning\n113\tImplementation exists (branch A, branch B, or the no-epic bring-your-own fallback), and it\n114\tresolves the scoping-model open question. It has downstream dependents and both branch epics are\n115\tproperly gated by it with `remove` semantics.\n116\t\n117\tGating metadata is complete and correct: every epic with a non-null `gated_by` (E005, both\n118\tbranches) has `gate_failure_impact` with both `action` and `fallback_approach` populated, and\n119\tevery `gated_by` target appears in that epic's direct `dependencies`. E005 lists an Investigation\n120\t(E001) in `dependencies` and correspondingly sets `gated_by` — no ungated Investigation\n121\tdependency anywhere.\n122\t\n123\tMinor: E004 is typed Investigation but nothing in the DAG depends on or is gated by it (its\n124\toutputs flow via dashed informational links and AC wording). The typing is nonetheless\n125\tdefensible — the epic resolves genuine uncertainty (is pgvector acceptable as the default, at\n126\twhat resource cost) whose failure path is \"revisit the default-store choice,\" and its sizing\n127\toutput adjusts what the provisioning epics build — but it is the weakest typing in the set; a\n128\tcase could be made for treating the benchmark harness as an Implementation with the analysis as\n129\tan AC.\n130\t\n131\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n132\t\n133\tEvery epic uses the signal set matching its type: E002/E003/E005/branches carry `ai_signals`\n134\t(9-signal rubric); E001/E004 carry `investigation_signals` (5-signal rubric). No cross-type\n135\tcontamination. All seven ai-signals rationale files exist and their rationales match the\n136\tfrontmatter values.\n137\t\n138\tSpot-checks confirm the values against the strategy content: E001's `source_accessibility: 0`\n139\tcorrectly reflects that the gating answer (RHAISTRAT-7164 landing in time) does not exist in any\n140\treadable artifact, and `human_judgment_required: -2` matches the Staff-directed sprint-planning\n141\tgate. Branch A appropriately diverges from Branch B (`existing_foundation: 0` and\n142\t`external_dependency: -1` for the undelivered operator layer vs `existing_foundation: 1` and\n143\t`open_questions: -1` for the lifecycle-design caveats in the BFF path). E005's\n144\t`human_process_gates: -1` matches the mandatory docs technical review. No signal value\n145\tcontradicts the rubric conditions or the strategy text.\n146\t\n147\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n148\t\n149\tEvery epic has concrete, testable ACs derived from the strategy and Staff input. Rule-mandated\n150\tACs: E005 (docs-authoring) has the technical-review-against-implementation AC; no konflux-chain\n151\tepics exist (N/A); no replacement epic requires a rollback/feature-flag AC — the inline Milvus\n152\tremoval is RHAISTRAT-7474's scope, and the pgvector provider is env-guard activated\n153\t(`ENABLE_PGVECTOR`), which is itself the activation switch. E004's ACs encode the Staff-defined\n154\tpass criterion (order of magnitude, no latency SLA). The branch epics include the\n155\tend-to-end RAG validation, per-namespace isolation, resource-sizing rationale, cleanup, and\n156\t(branch B) restart idempotency ACs — directly traceable to the Staff Engineer caveats. No epic\n157\tbody references sibling epics by draft ID; cross-epic references use work descriptions or real\n158\tticket keys (RHAISTRAT-7164, RHAISTRAT-7474).\n159\t\n160\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n161\t\n162\tAll strategy scope after Staff-Engineer filtering is accounted for: AC-1 (branch epics), AC-2\n163\t(E002/E003/E004), AC-4 (E002/E003), AC-5 (E001 decision + branches), AC-6 (E004 sizing +\n164\tbranches); both strategy open questions are resolved into explicit scope (scoping model into\n165\tE001's decision record; migration path into E003's configurable connection plus E005\n166\tdocumentation); Staff corrections are carried as documented overrides rather than dropped (no\n167\timage build/Konflux scope, vector_io-only configuration, SQLite untouched, Auto Rag decoupled,\n168\tperformance thresholds removed, model-registry sizing reference). Conditional branches cover all\n169\tthree bounded outcomes of the go/no-go: branch A (go), branch B (no-go, BFF-managed), and the\n170\tbring-your-own outcome correctly modeled as a no-epic fallback recorded in branch B's\n171\t`gate_failure_impact` and covered by E003 + E005. Cross-epic consistency holds: E002's provider,\n172\tE003's wiring, and the branches' provisioning collectively account for every item in each\n173\tother's scope via the connection-secret contract. A docs-authoring epic is present. Strategy-level\n174\tcontext (risks, release-commitment risk, scope traps) is carried forward in Health Warnings\n175\trather than silently dropped.\n176\t\n177\tMinor: completeness could not be verified against the unavailable strategy attachment (NFRs,\n178\tdependency table, affected-components table); the decomposition flags this itself and recommends\n179\thuman reconciliation before execution.\n180\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-82d6bdc1eb179fe2","agent_id":"ac66c1a55ffc2a70d","session_id":"546378e3-e46e-4fda-8937-a306cf6cc87c","uuid":"4922870d-1f5a-4074-a848-7f194adbc6e7","timestamp":"2026-10-21T19:13:56.073Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-9f9a57e06586b6b0","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
